and I'm actually going to

00:01:45.606 --> 00:01:48.110
use or I'm going to
tee off this slide,

00:01:48.111 --> 00:01:53.100
use this slide to tee off
my final closing thoughts.

00:01:53.101 --> 00:01:57.565
So, the way we at Microsoft

00:01:57.566 --> 00:02:00.830
mostly think about
this slide and you've heard

00:02:00.831 --> 00:02:03.850
this slogan many times,

00:02:03.851 --> 00:02:06.760
is intelligent edge
intelligent cloud.

00:02:06.761 --> 00:02:08.450
So, we essentially say well,

00:02:08.451 --> 00:02:09.750
there's this public cloud,

00:02:09.751 --> 00:02:11.780
everything that we don't fully

00:02:11.781 --> 00:02:15.275
understand and has some shape
right in this cloud form,

00:02:15.276 --> 00:02:16.560
maybe even private cloud,

00:02:16.561 --> 00:02:18.880
that's our Intelligent Cloud,

00:02:18.881 --> 00:02:22.830
abundant computing and
whatsoever and we put it there.

00:02:22.831 --> 00:02:27.485
Then we have all these devices
and our mobile phones,

00:02:27.486 --> 00:02:31.340
our toy cars that are connected

00:02:31.341 --> 00:02:33.110
to the Internet and
that's what we call

00:02:33.111 --> 00:02:35.985
the Intelligent Edge, right?

00:02:35.986 --> 00:02:40.855
We also reason about
it and why we do this.

00:02:40.856 --> 00:02:42.550
We have the Intelligent Edge,

00:02:42.551 --> 00:02:44.545
is where we collect the data,

00:02:44.546 --> 00:02:47.255
that's where we make decisions

00:02:47.256 --> 00:02:50.405
because the decisions have
to be implemented fast,

00:02:50.406 --> 00:02:53.180
and also this is the environment

00:02:53.181 --> 00:02:54.640
we trust a little bit more.

00:02:54.641 --> 00:02:56.160
Our phone is with us.

00:02:56.161 --> 00:02:57.380
We trust it a little bit more.

00:02:57.381 --> 00:02:58.830
Of course it can be hacked.

00:02:58.831 --> 00:03:01.755
But but it's close

00:03:01.756 --> 00:03:05.115
to us whereas the cloud
is somehow remote.

00:03:05.116 --> 00:03:08.530
The cloud is where we
have abundant computing.

00:03:08.531 --> 00:03:11.565
We typically don't
trust what's going on.

00:03:11.566 --> 00:03:13.500
It has higher latency,

00:03:13.501 --> 00:03:17.015
but that's where we
do data analytics.

00:03:17.016 --> 00:03:18.940
That's where we do training.

00:03:18.941 --> 00:03:21.485
So, that's how we mostly think

00:03:21.486 --> 00:03:24.885
about this world with all the,

00:03:24.886 --> 00:03:27.765
I almost pressed
the red button here but I

00:03:27.766 --> 00:03:31.110
didn't have to go back one slide.

00:03:31.111 --> 00:03:33.950
So, this is how we mostly
think about the world and

00:03:33.951 --> 00:03:37.700
it's nicely petition and so on.

00:03:37.701 --> 00:03:41.150
So my final thought is
that actually because of

00:03:41.151 --> 00:03:42.920
what Albert was talking about

00:03:42.921 --> 00:03:45.860
all this cable and
hyper-connectivity,

00:03:45.861 --> 00:03:49.010
I think we now have
something else.

00:03:49.011 --> 00:03:52.584
Essentially, this is
all moving together,

00:03:52.585 --> 00:03:56.720
and it's transforming to

00:03:56.721 --> 00:04:00.710
one big supercomputer
that we sometimes and

00:04:00.711 --> 00:04:02.570
you might have heard
that term as well

00:04:02.571 --> 00:04:05.590
called the global
AI supercomputer.

00:04:05.591 --> 00:04:08.420
This is like think of everything

00:04:08.421 --> 00:04:10.640
all the computing
devices somehow they're

00:04:10.641 --> 00:04:13.100
all connected through
the Internet,

00:04:13.101 --> 00:04:15.270
through the same logical fabric.

00:04:15.271 --> 00:04:17.270
Of course there is
their virtual nets

00:04:17.271 --> 00:04:19.375
as Albert mentioned yesterday,

00:04:19.376 --> 00:04:21.425
but somehow they're
all connected,

00:04:21.426 --> 00:04:24.070
and this is one big machine.

00:04:24.071 --> 00:04:27.410
I think this is the picture

00:04:27.411 --> 00:04:30.920
or the metaphor that I
would like to use for

00:04:30.921 --> 00:04:34.415
these brief closing
remarks before

00:04:34.416 --> 00:04:36.910
Sandy catches me off

00:04:36.911 --> 00:04:41.270
the stage because he wants
to go home, I assume.

00:04:41.271 --> 00:04:44.840
We all want to go home.
So, there are essentially

00:04:44.841 --> 00:04:48.875
two perspectives on
this supercomputer.

00:04:48.876 --> 00:04:51.440
There's the perspective that

00:04:51.441 --> 00:04:53.270
we probably are familiar with,

00:04:53.271 --> 00:04:57.080
is the internal perspective
where we essentially look at

00:04:57.081 --> 00:04:59.570
the components at each

00:04:59.571 --> 00:05:03.160
of a set of components
of this supercomputer.

00:05:03.161 --> 00:05:05.720
Let's say, a drone that

00:05:05.721 --> 00:05:09.285
communicates with
a mobile phone for example.

00:05:09.286 --> 00:05:12.100
But there's also
the external perspective,

00:05:12.101 --> 00:05:15.295
where we really look at
this as one machine.

00:05:15.296 --> 00:05:17.885
Just think of it
this is one machine.

00:05:17.886 --> 00:05:21.390
Just as today, your phone or

00:05:21.391 --> 00:05:25.470
even your car is not
just one CPU process.

00:05:25.471 --> 00:05:29.480
So your car probably
has 100 CPUs inside,

00:05:29.481 --> 00:05:34.695
and your PC has a GPU inside,

00:05:34.696 --> 00:05:37.230
has several cores inside,

00:05:37.231 --> 00:05:41.275
in future it might have
some accelerators inside.

00:05:41.276 --> 00:05:45.205
So, just think of this now
as this one big machine.

00:05:45.206 --> 00:05:48.725
Yeah? The important
thing and I'll

00:05:48.726 --> 00:05:52.220
come to that I'll first go to
the internal perspectives,

00:05:52.221 --> 00:05:54.110
that most of the people
in the world are

00:05:54.111 --> 00:05:56.740
not Systems Researchers like us.

00:05:56.741 --> 00:05:59.365
Yeah? So, Systems Researchers

00:05:59.366 --> 00:06:02.670
look at the components and
somehow how they interact,

00:06:02.671 --> 00:06:05.175
but most of the
people on this planet

00:06:05.176 --> 00:06:08.405
look at the experience
that they have

00:06:08.406 --> 00:06:11.300
with computing as a kind of

00:06:11.301 --> 00:06:14.900
one experience as one machine.

00:06:14.901 --> 00:06:19.790
In the old days it was a PC
and then it was a phone,

00:06:19.791 --> 00:06:21.680
but now it is really all of

00:06:21.681 --> 00:06:24.220
these devices that create
these experiences.

00:06:24.221 --> 00:06:26.360
So, I want to go
a little bit through

00:06:26.361 --> 00:06:28.970
these two perspectives and

00:06:28.971 --> 00:06:30.900
start with the
internal perspective,

00:06:30.901 --> 00:06:34.700
because most of the talks
that we had here and most of

00:06:34.701 --> 00:06:36.800
the discussion that we

00:06:36.801 --> 00:06:40.110
had here was really on
the internal perspective.

00:06:40.111 --> 00:06:43.710
That is great to see and
it's all great work.

00:06:43.711 --> 00:06:45.740
This is what our everyday job

00:06:45.741 --> 00:06:46.880
and it's a lot of fun and we

00:06:46.881 --> 00:06:48.410
should definitely and most

00:06:48.411 --> 00:06:50.385
definitely continue that, right?

00:06:50.386 --> 00:06:53.945
So, all the work we
have done in improving

00:06:53.946 --> 00:06:56.885
components or connections or

00:06:56.886 --> 00:06:59.894
pieces of this big supercomputer,

00:06:59.895 --> 00:07:02.930
those are still important
and highly relevant and we

00:07:02.931 --> 00:07:06.740
still want to keep
that work alive.

00:07:06.741 --> 00:07:11.900
There are a lot of new
ideas and that is why I

00:07:11.901 --> 00:07:17.525
felt the summit was so exciting,

00:07:17.526 --> 00:07:19.490
because even though
that we've been

00:07:19.491 --> 00:07:21.830
doing this for I
don't know 50 years,

00:07:21.831 --> 00:07:24.680
60 years since for
nine months or so,

00:07:24.681 --> 00:07:26.210
obviously I haven't
been doing it for

00:07:26.211 --> 00:07:28.330
60 years and most of you not.

00:07:28.331 --> 00:07:30.770
There is continuously new ideas

00:07:30.771 --> 00:07:32.350
exciting ideas to do this,

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:34.250
and we've heard many of them.

00:07:34.251 --> 00:07:38.480
There was a whole track
on AI for systems.

00:07:38.481 --> 00:07:41.190
There was work on how
to predict workloads,

00:07:41.191 --> 00:07:44.074
how to predict what
is the best decision,

00:07:44.075 --> 00:07:47.540
and this is really exciting
and that didn't definitely

00:07:47.541 --> 00:07:51.340
not exist five or
even three years ago,

00:07:51.341 --> 00:07:53.180
this kind of thinking and work.

00:07:53.181 --> 00:07:56.915
There is this whole line
of work of synthesizing

00:07:56.916 --> 00:08:00.700
code or instance
optimized system.

00:08:00.701 --> 00:08:02.945
Yesterday there was this session

00:08:02.946 --> 00:08:05.550
which was talking about
learned indexes where

00:08:05.551 --> 00:08:07.910
you use machine learning

00:08:07.911 --> 00:08:11.100
to create data structures
and algorithms.

00:08:11.101 --> 00:08:14.980
There was talk
about software 2.0,

00:08:14.981 --> 00:08:17.780
a very new way to
create software which

00:08:17.781 --> 00:08:20.870
actually I'll come to and might
actually become relevant.

00:08:20.871 --> 00:08:22.550
There are systems for AI.

00:08:22.551 --> 00:08:25.140
We now have a, I'm
a database person.

00:08:25.141 --> 00:08:27.230
So, we optimize database systems

00:08:27.231 --> 00:08:30.135
because we know the operations
that we need to do.

00:08:30.136 --> 00:08:33.840
With AI you also know
the operations we need to do.

00:08:33.841 --> 00:08:36.030
We had a lot of this in

00:08:36.031 --> 00:08:38.250
several talks during

00:08:38.251 --> 00:08:40.590
those faculty summit
focusing on that.

00:08:40.591 --> 00:08:43.160
This is a whole new great kind of

00:08:43.161 --> 00:08:46.750
mining pool for ideas
and great work to be.

00:08:46.751 --> 00:08:49.260
Custom hardware, security, we

00:08:49.261 --> 00:08:52.710
talked a lot about enclaves,

00:08:52.711 --> 00:08:57.215
about Intel SGX or
even Hyper- FPGAs,

00:08:57.216 --> 00:09:02.050
hardware software co-design,
cross-layer optimizations.

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:04.940
Some of these ideas are very

00:09:04.941 --> 00:09:07.220
new and some of these ideas are

00:09:07.221 --> 00:09:08.960
old and done in

00:09:08.961 --> 00:09:11.625
a new context and all
that is very exciting,

00:09:11.626 --> 00:09:13.755
and we should
continue to do that.

00:09:13.756 --> 00:09:15.620
But what I would like to focus

00:09:15.621 --> 00:09:17.795
on is the external perspective,

00:09:17.796 --> 00:09:22.045
what really society expects
from us or the what,

00:09:22.046 --> 00:09:24.610
I mean what we
usually say as what

00:09:24.611 --> 00:09:28.975
my grandmother expects
from me to deliver.

00:09:28.976 --> 00:09:32.360
So the first, we're
creating or I mean we

00:09:32.361 --> 00:09:35.500
are a big part of creating
this big machine,

00:09:35.501 --> 00:09:40.550
and the first question
that society asks us,

00:09:40.551 --> 00:09:42.730
what is the value
of this machine?

00:09:42.731 --> 00:09:45.220
I think we should also

00:09:45.221 --> 00:09:49.170
step back and think
about that as well,

00:09:49.171 --> 00:09:50.470
and there's all these questions.

00:09:50.471 --> 00:09:53.460
So, those are technical
questions as well.

00:09:53.461 --> 00:09:56.270
How do I program this machine?

00:09:56.660 --> 00:09:59.985
How do I use this machine?

00:09:59.986 --> 00:10:03.690
With my telephone, with
my smartphone it's very clear.

00:10:03.691 --> 00:10:04.920
With my PC it's

00:10:04.921 --> 00:10:08.095
a screen it's the keyboard,
it has speakers.

00:10:08.096 --> 00:10:10.930
The interaction modalities
are very clear.

00:10:10.931 --> 00:10:14.180
Here, there's so many
interaction modalities

00:10:14.181 --> 00:10:15.615
and they keep on growing.

00:10:15.616 --> 00:10:18.020
The programming and
the interaction

00:10:18.021 --> 00:10:20.009
becomes very, very interesting,

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:22.610
because of course we

00:10:22.611 --> 00:10:25.580
want to program
experiences for the user,

00:10:25.581 --> 00:10:28.400
but what is the set of
the devices that the user

00:10:28.401 --> 00:10:31.710
has available for the experience
that I want to program?

00:10:31.711 --> 00:10:36.695
I know very little
about the context and

00:10:36.696 --> 00:10:39.990
even the computational context

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:43.770
of my application for my user,

00:10:43.771 --> 00:10:49.580
and that is obviously going
to make my programming-

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:54.020
My task of programming
application were difficult.

00:10:54.021 --> 00:10:55.690
Every user, probably there are

00:10:55.691 --> 00:10:58.820
no two users in
this room that have

00:10:58.821 --> 00:11:00.760
exactly the same set of

00:11:00.761 --> 00:11:05.410
devices in their
normal everyday life.

00:11:05.411 --> 00:11:09.535
Then of course,
the big questions are;

00:11:09.536 --> 00:11:11.760
what can I do with

00:11:11.761 --> 00:11:14.850
this machine that I
couldn't do before?

00:11:14.851 --> 00:11:17.920
This is not only
for us and I'll get

00:11:17.921 --> 00:11:20.790
to answer that question
because most

00:11:20.791 --> 00:11:23.340
of the really interesting
things that we're

00:11:23.341 --> 00:11:26.460
doing are solving
the world's biggest problems,

00:11:26.461 --> 00:11:27.530
climate change, and so on.

00:11:27.531 --> 00:11:29.705
It's not that we
can do this alone,

00:11:29.706 --> 00:11:32.430
even as computer
science community,

00:11:32.431 --> 00:11:34.730
but of course we
have to reach out to

00:11:34.731 --> 00:11:38.360
other scientific disciplines and

00:11:38.361 --> 00:11:41.465
also to the rest of society.

00:11:41.466 --> 00:11:45.840
Then of course, maybe one of

00:11:45.841 --> 00:11:48.420
the most exciting questions
for us and I'm not saying

00:11:48.421 --> 00:11:51.610
that these are easy to
answer questions is;

00:11:51.611 --> 00:11:53.645
what can't I do?

00:11:53.646 --> 00:11:56.105
And what I would like to do?

00:11:56.106 --> 00:11:58.720
So, that's the first thing I

00:11:58.721 --> 00:12:02.570
think that we have to deliver.

00:12:02.571 --> 00:12:04.095
The second thing of course,

00:12:04.096 --> 00:12:05.815
that's the plus side,

00:12:05.816 --> 00:12:09.280
and we all know that
society expects from us

00:12:09.281 --> 00:12:13.610
also to very carefully
think about the minus side.

00:12:13.611 --> 00:12:16.160
So, we really have to think

00:12:16.161 --> 00:12:18.280
about the risk and
its security was

00:12:18.281 --> 00:12:23.195
a huge topic here over
these last two days.

00:12:23.196 --> 00:12:24.975
So, what can go wrong?

00:12:24.976 --> 00:12:26.760
How do I hack this machine?

00:12:26.761 --> 00:12:28.785
Of course, this machine,

00:12:28.786 --> 00:12:30.875
every year, there are

00:12:30.876 --> 00:12:33.880
nine billion new
micro-controllers produced.

00:12:33.881 --> 00:12:36.470
So, every year, we are producing

00:12:36.471 --> 00:12:40.020
at least nine billion
attack points,

00:12:40.021 --> 00:12:41.980
and so, we can leak

00:12:41.981 --> 00:12:45.155
information that we
don't want to leak.

00:12:45.156 --> 00:12:48.150
Somebody can change
our information.

00:12:48.151 --> 00:12:50.210
I'm not particularly
happy if somebody,

00:12:50.211 --> 00:12:52.810
like I want to go

00:12:52.811 --> 00:12:55.430
to the Olympic Peninsula
on the weekend,

00:12:55.431 --> 00:12:57.015
I've booked the ferry boat,

00:12:57.016 --> 00:12:58.865
and if somebody
changes the ticket

00:12:58.866 --> 00:13:01.255
of my ferry boat
without me noticing,

00:13:01.256 --> 00:13:03.305
I'm not going to be very happy.

00:13:03.306 --> 00:13:05.140
A lot of this,

00:13:05.141 --> 00:13:06.670
if you think about it,

00:13:06.671 --> 00:13:09.040
it's going to be one of

00:13:09.041 --> 00:13:12.190
the big questions is about
how to compose security.

00:13:12.191 --> 00:13:14.800
We haven't talked
much about that here,

00:13:14.801 --> 00:13:16.840
but it is something that we at

00:13:16.841 --> 00:13:19.920
Microsoft really
struggle with greatly,

00:13:19.921 --> 00:13:26.100
because we have many great ideas
to many great protocols,

00:13:26.101 --> 00:13:28.380
and many great ideas to solve

00:13:28.381 --> 00:13:33.115
a specific security problem
in a particular context.

00:13:33.116 --> 00:13:35.250
But now, if you put a puzzle,

00:13:35.251 --> 00:13:37.190
all of these contexts together,

00:13:37.191 --> 00:13:40.500
and look at different
paths through it,

00:13:40.501 --> 00:13:44.095
that's where the big problems
come in security.

00:13:44.096 --> 00:13:47.805
Of course, there's
a whole regulation aspect,

00:13:47.806 --> 00:13:50.940
how do we as Microsoft
as a big problem for

00:13:50.941 --> 00:13:54.630
us prove that we are GDPR
compliant for example?

00:13:54.631 --> 00:13:57.315
We want to be GDPR compliant.

00:13:57.316 --> 00:13:59.100
It's really something, and we're

00:13:59.101 --> 00:14:01.300
doing huge efforts to do that,

00:14:01.301 --> 00:14:03.480
but of course, we want
to prove and we're proud

00:14:03.481 --> 00:14:04.620
of all the efforts that we're

00:14:04.621 --> 00:14:05.950
doing. We want to prove that.

00:14:05.951 --> 00:14:07.460
On the other side, of course,

00:14:07.461 --> 00:14:09.685
we want to understand regulators

00:14:09.686 --> 00:14:15.330
and when they specify

00:14:15.331 --> 00:14:19.680
regulations like GDPR,
and other regulations.

00:14:19.681 --> 00:14:24.490
Of course, there's
the normal systems aspect

00:14:24.491 --> 00:14:26.410
of this and so
there's a plus side,

00:14:26.411 --> 00:14:27.855
a minus side, and of course,

00:14:27.856 --> 00:14:30.470
we need to optimize this machine.

00:14:30.471 --> 00:14:34.100
Of course, any of us
work on this, again,

00:14:34.101 --> 00:14:37.645
at different aspects and
that's where things again,

00:14:37.646 --> 00:14:41.190
if you optimize
this machine at some point,

00:14:41.191 --> 00:14:43.860
that might not matter
in the bigger context.

00:14:43.861 --> 00:14:47.290
But really, now computing

00:14:47.291 --> 00:14:50.795
is the biggest energy
consumer on the planet,

00:14:50.796 --> 00:14:54.290
and you know all
about these stories

00:14:54.291 --> 00:14:59.620
about that Bitcoin and Ethereum,

00:14:59.621 --> 00:15:01.245
the proof of work.

00:15:01.246 --> 00:15:04.800
This is a major
sustainability and

00:15:04.801 --> 00:15:08.295
climate change is starting to

00:15:08.296 --> 00:15:11.330
be a factor because of

00:15:11.331 --> 00:15:15.610
the economics of consuming
energy in this thing.

00:15:15.611 --> 00:15:18.505
So, we have a huge
responsibility here to

00:15:18.506 --> 00:15:22.320
optimize for the
sustainability of the planet.

00:15:22.321 --> 00:15:26.850
So, there are
all these other questions

00:15:26.851 --> 00:15:31.150
because this is a machine
that is not owned by anybody.

00:15:31.151 --> 00:15:34.560
It's owned by all of us and

00:15:34.561 --> 00:15:37.865
some pieces might be owned
by different people.

00:15:37.866 --> 00:15:41.760
Microsoft might have
a fairly big share

00:15:41.761 --> 00:15:43.810
because it has many servers,

00:15:43.811 --> 00:15:46.020
but overall, this is still tiny

00:15:46.021 --> 00:15:50.080
compared to the magnitude
of this big machine.

00:15:50.081 --> 00:15:53.430
So, all of these
questions are pretty

00:15:53.431 --> 00:15:56.905
big and it's not really,

00:15:56.906 --> 00:15:58.530
yeah, you would say,

00:15:58.531 --> 00:16:01.770
okay, that's a good
research topic but then,

00:16:01.771 --> 00:16:04.220
if you think about
it five minutes,

00:16:04.221 --> 00:16:07.390
you'll say, well,
really, what can I do?

00:16:07.391 --> 00:16:09.520
So, what I'm going to do is,

00:16:09.521 --> 00:16:11.805
I'm just going to give
you some examples

00:16:11.806 --> 00:16:14.460
how we at Microsoft
Research have been

00:16:14.461 --> 00:16:18.585
going about this addressing

00:16:18.586 --> 00:16:20.465
some of these questions in a way.

00:16:20.466 --> 00:16:23.340
So, we don't have
access to this machine.

00:16:23.341 --> 00:16:28.225
It's woven into our societies
and into our planet,

00:16:28.226 --> 00:16:30.450
and into our everyday lives.

00:16:30.451 --> 00:16:32.060
We cannot really access it.

00:16:32.061 --> 00:16:37.405
It's invisible, and
it's not a test beds.

00:16:37.406 --> 00:16:40.795
We cannot put it into a lab
and replicate it in the lab.

00:16:40.796 --> 00:16:45.780
Even with all the Cloud computing

00:16:45.781 --> 00:16:47.835
and simulation that we have,

00:16:47.836 --> 00:16:49.600
even that, we cannot simulate

00:16:49.601 --> 00:16:51.795
it and we cannot
replicate it in a lab.

00:16:51.796 --> 00:16:54.190
So, the way to
really go about it,

00:16:54.191 --> 00:16:56.980
is to take a slice
of this machine.

00:16:56.981 --> 00:17:00.600
That's a very different
thinking that

00:17:00.601 --> 00:17:04.355
we have typically in
systems research,

00:17:04.356 --> 00:17:06.110
where in systems research,

00:17:06.111 --> 00:17:08.760
you usually take
a vertical slice.

00:17:08.761 --> 00:17:10.195
You look at the stack,

00:17:10.196 --> 00:17:13.450
and then you take a piece,

00:17:13.451 --> 00:17:16.465
a vertical slice of the stack.

00:17:16.466 --> 00:17:20.980
But, what I'm going to talk
about or what I'm going to

00:17:20.981 --> 00:17:25.730
give examples are slices that go,

00:17:25.731 --> 00:17:28.330
not necessary vertically
and horizontally,

00:17:28.331 --> 00:17:34.545
they go in zigzag through
what this machine is about.

00:17:34.546 --> 00:17:36.765
I'm going to start
with one example,

00:17:36.766 --> 00:17:39.120
which might be surprising
here, but I'll do it,

00:17:39.121 --> 00:17:42.840
which is how we thought
about education and one of

00:17:42.841 --> 00:17:47.140
the examples where
Microsoft engaged.

00:17:47.141 --> 00:17:49.820
All of these examples are
also examples that are not

00:17:49.821 --> 00:17:52.840
MS internal or even
Microsoft Internal.

00:17:52.841 --> 00:17:55.750
They are all examples where
we reached out with somebody

00:17:55.751 --> 00:18:00.105
else in the world,
mostly in academia.

00:18:00.106 --> 00:18:03.030
So, the first one
was about education.

00:18:03.031 --> 00:18:04.960
The reason why I'm saying this,

00:18:04.961 --> 00:18:10.300
this is an interesting piece
of schooling hardware.

00:18:10.301 --> 00:18:12.845
This Microbit, I don't
know who has heard of it.

00:18:12.846 --> 00:18:17.790
It's a very simple processor
with some interactions.

00:18:17.791 --> 00:18:22.175
It has some lights and it
has a little bit of sensing.

00:18:22.176 --> 00:18:27.100
This is a piece of
hardware that BBC in

00:18:27.101 --> 00:18:29.460
the UK has advertised

00:18:29.461 --> 00:18:31.780
and has shipped to
schools and is available,

00:18:31.781 --> 00:18:34.330
you can buy it,
you can get a kit.

00:18:34.331 --> 00:18:36.025
But it also comes,

00:18:36.026 --> 00:18:37.880
and this is the right side
of that picture,

00:18:37.881 --> 00:18:39.325
with a programming model.

00:18:39.326 --> 00:18:43.385
So, it's education
how to bring people,

00:18:43.386 --> 00:18:47.460
how to convince
people and children,

00:18:47.461 --> 00:18:49.675
in this case really children,

00:18:49.676 --> 00:18:52.690
how to make them
program of course,

00:18:52.691 --> 00:18:55.135
in a playful manner.

00:18:55.136 --> 00:18:57.320
This is not real applications,

00:18:57.321 --> 00:18:59.075
and this is really not going to

00:18:59.076 --> 00:19:02.705
solve climate change
this application.

00:19:02.706 --> 00:19:05.550
But they learn
programming through

00:19:05.551 --> 00:19:07.780
a Web interface through
a graphical thing

00:19:07.781 --> 00:19:10.240
and I'm showing a little bit
some piece of code,

00:19:10.241 --> 00:19:11.690
and then they can run it,

00:19:11.691 --> 00:19:15.440
and they can experience a device.

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:20.595
The concept is called
Physical Programming.

00:19:20.596 --> 00:19:24.040
It's one way how hardware with

00:19:24.041 --> 00:19:27.555
software engineering
somehow comes together,

00:19:27.556 --> 00:19:29.270
hardware design with
software engineering,

00:19:29.271 --> 00:19:32.855
to create something in
the educational space.

00:19:32.856 --> 00:19:35.130
I'm going to give
you another example

00:19:35.131 --> 00:19:36.730
that we had this
morning, actually,

00:19:36.731 --> 00:19:38.640
heard it twice this
morning because of

00:19:38.641 --> 00:19:41.380
technical issues and
it's exactly that slide.

00:19:41.381 --> 00:19:44.220
Of course, I stole that slide,

00:19:44.221 --> 00:19:46.780
I'm allowed to do that
as a Lab Director,

00:19:46.781 --> 00:19:48.290
is the FarmBeats project.

00:19:48.291 --> 00:19:50.530
Again, my point here is,

00:19:50.531 --> 00:19:54.410
it is motivated by
a real-world problem and then,

00:19:54.411 --> 00:19:58.620
it looks and it changes
through the components

00:19:58.621 --> 00:20:02.990
that are needed in
this global AI supercomputer,

00:20:02.991 --> 00:20:04.505
and there's a lot of AI here,

00:20:04.506 --> 00:20:06.995
but there's a lot of
systems work in here.

00:20:06.996 --> 00:20:09.080
So, what essentially happens is,

00:20:09.081 --> 00:20:13.635
the drone flies over
a field, take pictures,

00:20:13.636 --> 00:20:18.270
these pictures are
a little bit pre-filter

00:20:18.271 --> 00:20:19.340
but they are going

00:20:19.341 --> 00:20:25.400
through this TV white spaces
network to some edge,

00:20:25.401 --> 00:20:30.310
device, or a gateway where
there are further process.

00:20:30.311 --> 00:20:31.710
There's some AI done,

00:20:31.711 --> 00:20:33.650
they then go to
the Cloud where there's

00:20:33.651 --> 00:20:36.335
a little bit more
comprehensive AI done

00:20:36.336 --> 00:20:40.220
in order to understand what
is going on in this field.

00:20:40.221 --> 00:20:41.950
What is the humidity,

00:20:41.951 --> 00:20:48.320
what is the water saturation
of the soil in that field?

00:20:48.770 --> 00:20:55.590
We have this demo
of how the FPGA can

00:20:55.591 --> 00:21:02.095
actually do this real-time AI
on image processing.

00:21:02.096 --> 00:21:04.655
Then, of course, it goes
back to the gateway,

00:21:04.656 --> 00:21:07.860
in order to make
local decisions at

00:21:07.861 --> 00:21:15.460
the gateway on the farm
on how to do watering,

00:21:15.461 --> 00:21:18.275
pesticides, and
chemicals on that field.

00:21:18.276 --> 00:21:19.960
Again, it is something that is

00:21:19.961 --> 00:21:23.660
motivated by a
real-world need somehow

00:21:23.661 --> 00:21:29.975
goes in a very complex way
through this AI supercomputer.

00:21:29.976 --> 00:21:33.010
By studying these, we learn

00:21:33.011 --> 00:21:36.330
a huge amount about
how to build systems,

00:21:36.331 --> 00:21:37.745
how to optimize them,

00:21:37.746 --> 00:21:41.290
and how to make what
I said yesterday,

00:21:41.291 --> 00:21:43.810
this one percent improvement,

00:21:43.811 --> 00:21:48.185
how to make that really
matter and valuable.

00:21:48.186 --> 00:21:51.130
I'm going to give
two more examples,

00:21:51.131 --> 00:21:53.460
the next example this is again

00:21:53.461 --> 00:21:56.310
is a chart that I didn't do,

00:21:56.311 --> 00:21:59.790
it's a typical one of
these industry block charts,

00:21:59.791 --> 00:22:01.655
but you really don't
have to understand it.

00:22:01.656 --> 00:22:03.315
I'm just going to
tell you the story,

00:22:03.316 --> 00:22:07.035
what this is about is a
water project in California.

00:22:07.036 --> 00:22:08.810
Again, it's a collaboration.

00:22:08.811 --> 00:22:10.270
FarmBeats is a collaboration

00:22:10.271 --> 00:22:13.550
with many agricultural companies,

00:22:13.551 --> 00:22:15.780
but also with
several universities.

00:22:15.781 --> 00:22:18.470
Just yesterday evening
I was talking to Hakim

00:22:18.471 --> 00:22:22.605
and Cornell who's actually,

00:22:22.606 --> 00:22:26.285
Cornell is also collaborating
as part of FarmBeats.

00:22:26.286 --> 00:22:29.100
This is a water
project in California

00:22:29.101 --> 00:22:30.380
a collaboration with

00:22:30.381 --> 00:22:33.365
the University of
California and Davis,

00:22:33.366 --> 00:22:41.855
who are the masterminds
of water planning.

00:22:41.856 --> 00:22:43.470
The problem there is really

00:22:43.471 --> 00:22:46.340
that you would think
that water is,

00:22:46.341 --> 00:22:48.730
well this is a simple problem

00:22:48.731 --> 00:22:50.540
you collect all the
data and that's it,

00:22:50.541 --> 00:22:55.390
but there are like 400 different
data sources that are

00:22:55.391 --> 00:22:57.535
needed to reason about

00:22:57.536 --> 00:23:01.175
water supply and
consumption in California.

00:23:01.176 --> 00:23:03.730
One of the big
problems is leakages

00:23:03.731 --> 00:23:06.450
where waste water because
some pipe is broken,

00:23:06.451 --> 00:23:08.270
but just finding that out sounds

00:23:08.271 --> 00:23:11.120
like technically from
our perspective easy but

00:23:11.121 --> 00:23:12.680
really to get there you have to

00:23:12.681 --> 00:23:16.350
integrate data from
multiple sources.

00:23:16.351 --> 00:23:18.950
As it is some

00:23:18.951 --> 00:23:21.435
of these sources don't
like to share their data,

00:23:21.436 --> 00:23:23.350
because they are
afraid that they're

00:23:23.351 --> 00:23:25.140
doing something wrong that is not

00:23:25.141 --> 00:23:26.730
compliant or because they have

00:23:26.731 --> 00:23:28.620
a competitive advantage as

00:23:28.621 --> 00:23:31.205
a water supplier in
their particular region.

00:23:31.206 --> 00:23:36.015
So what this really just shows
is the flow of the data,

00:23:36.016 --> 00:23:38.500
how you kind of integrate it in

00:23:38.501 --> 00:23:43.710
a trusted way so that
not every party,

00:23:43.711 --> 00:23:45.520
how do you then analyze it?

00:23:45.521 --> 00:23:48.060
Build different applications
and then how you

00:23:48.061 --> 00:23:50.920
make at the very
right side some of

00:23:50.921 --> 00:23:59.955
those results again openly
available to make decisions,

00:23:59.956 --> 00:24:03.835
governmental decisions in
California about water.

00:24:03.836 --> 00:24:06.380
The last example I want to show

00:24:06.381 --> 00:24:09.609
is one that I'm
involved in myself,

00:24:09.610 --> 00:24:11.460
and I'm saying it because we've

00:24:11.461 --> 00:24:13.970
been talking about Blockchain.

00:24:13.971 --> 00:24:16.955
I think everybody knows
this cartoon from,

00:24:16.956 --> 00:24:20.185
I think it's from the early 90s.

00:24:20.186 --> 00:24:22.035
It's a New Yorker,

00:24:22.036 --> 00:24:26.130
on the Internet nobody knows
that you're a dog, right?

00:24:27.330 --> 00:24:30.095
I love this cartoon for,

00:24:30.096 --> 00:24:33.870
because I think I'm a dog
and I was always very

00:24:33.871 --> 00:24:38.440
happy that the Internet
didn't know who I really was.

00:24:38.441 --> 00:24:42.150
But there are cases where
you essentially do,

00:24:42.151 --> 00:24:43.520
want to do something on

00:24:43.521 --> 00:24:45.950
the Internet or you do
want to do something

00:24:45.951 --> 00:24:48.550
digitally and you want

00:24:48.551 --> 00:24:51.500
the other side to know
really this is me.

00:24:51.501 --> 00:24:54.110
I'm actually going
through a very weird.

00:24:54.111 --> 00:24:58.990
So IRS, I did my tax
return everything

00:24:58.991 --> 00:25:04.390
is fine but IRS believes
that I'm spamming,

00:25:04.391 --> 00:25:07.385
that I stole my own identity.

00:25:07.386 --> 00:25:09.815
So, they didn't want to
pay me back my return,

00:25:09.816 --> 00:25:13.025
and now they are trying to

00:25:13.026 --> 00:25:17.280
somehow find a way that I can
convince them that I'm me.

00:25:17.281 --> 00:25:21.620
So, this is actually an
interesting problem for the IRS,

00:25:21.621 --> 00:25:25.240
but it's also and
maybe more interesting problem

00:25:25.241 --> 00:25:26.870
in a much more mundane thing.

00:25:26.871 --> 00:25:30.610
So let's say, think of the top,

00:25:30.611 --> 00:25:32.645
I'm the second to the left,

00:25:32.646 --> 00:25:35.110
I'm a person I have a phone

00:25:35.111 --> 00:25:38.590
and I want to maybe not
even on the Internet,

00:25:38.591 --> 00:25:41.280
I want to buy a glass of

00:25:41.281 --> 00:25:45.090
champagne and I want to
pay for it digitally.

00:25:45.091 --> 00:25:47.920
I want to somehow let's assume,

00:25:47.921 --> 00:25:51.340
I forgot my driver's
license, my paper,

00:25:51.341 --> 00:25:52.660
I want to somehow prove

00:25:52.661 --> 00:25:56.820
digitally that I
am older than 21.

00:25:58.700 --> 00:26:01.640
He wouldn't question
that I'm older than 21,

00:26:01.641 --> 00:26:05.350
but let's just assume that he is

00:26:05.351 --> 00:26:08.050
a very kind bar tender
that wants to

00:26:08.051 --> 00:26:11.505
kind of be nice to me
and ask me and cause me.

00:26:11.506 --> 00:26:17.630
So, that is the case so
essentially today you really

00:26:17.631 --> 00:26:19.150
have to have the driver's license

00:26:19.151 --> 00:26:20.610
there's no way for me to

00:26:20.611 --> 00:26:23.670
prove digitally
that I'm 21, right?

00:26:23.671 --> 00:26:25.320
That I can drink alcohol,

00:26:25.321 --> 00:26:27.820
and because online
casinos might have

00:26:27.821 --> 00:26:29.080
this problem but
they probably don't

00:26:29.081 --> 00:26:31.000
care that much
about this problem.

00:26:31.001 --> 00:26:34.775
So, there is a government
that issues IDs,

00:26:34.776 --> 00:26:36.450
there is a bank that is

00:26:36.451 --> 00:26:39.240
issue IDs credit cards are in

00:26:39.241 --> 00:26:43.290
some sense IDs that
are issued by banks,

00:26:44.780 --> 00:26:47.560
but we want to do
this all digital.

00:26:47.561 --> 00:26:49.360
In China, it's all digital,

00:26:49.361 --> 00:26:51.690
in the way that Alibaba or

00:26:51.691 --> 00:26:54.000
$0.10 they issue the IDs

00:26:54.001 --> 00:26:56.060
that you do for banking
and nobody else.

00:26:56.061 --> 00:26:58.640
But we really don't want
to do that in our culture,

00:26:58.641 --> 00:27:00.200
we don't want to trust

00:27:00.201 --> 00:27:05.895
just one provider like Alibaba
to issue IDs for payments.

00:27:05.896 --> 00:27:08.680
So, the idea here, and again,

00:27:08.681 --> 00:27:13.355
it's a problem motivated
and it's open.

00:27:13.356 --> 00:27:17.540
This whole work is
a standard by W3C,

00:27:17.541 --> 00:27:20.780
but it has many computational
and systems aspects

00:27:20.781 --> 00:27:22.410
that I want to get to that.

00:27:22.411 --> 00:27:23.880
So what is happening here?

00:27:23.881 --> 00:27:25.310
Essentially we are creating

00:27:25.311 --> 00:27:27.170
these digital IDs everybody can

00:27:27.171 --> 00:27:29.705
create digital IDs anytime

00:27:29.706 --> 00:27:31.880
and they are stored
in Blockchains.

00:27:31.881 --> 00:27:35.335
If there is a government ID,

00:27:35.336 --> 00:27:38.765
well, it will be digitally
signed by the government.

00:27:38.766 --> 00:27:41.895
The government can issue
it, can give it to me,

00:27:41.896 --> 00:27:43.990
and I can choose
to participate in

00:27:43.991 --> 00:27:46.080
this very open system and

00:27:46.081 --> 00:27:47.440
a Blockchain system think of

00:27:47.441 --> 00:27:50.710
like Bitcoin and
just put it there.

00:27:50.900 --> 00:27:53.490
What I put there is not

00:27:53.491 --> 00:27:56.550
my driver's license with
all the information,

00:27:56.551 --> 00:27:59.325
what I would really put
there is just a hash.

00:27:59.326 --> 00:28:02.690
Plus, I will put
there something and

00:28:02.691 --> 00:28:07.340
a pointer that points to
that signed document.

00:28:07.341 --> 00:28:12.310
So, think of all these IDs

00:28:12.311 --> 00:28:15.155
or hashes of these IDs
in the block chain,

00:28:15.156 --> 00:28:16.630
they will point to

00:28:16.631 --> 00:28:18.815
some other distributed database

00:28:18.816 --> 00:28:20.960
that will have again hashes

00:28:20.961 --> 00:28:23.710
of where you can look up

00:28:23.711 --> 00:28:25.170
those documents so that I can

00:28:25.171 --> 00:28:27.615
prove digitally with
my driver's license,

00:28:27.616 --> 00:28:29.970
but still no information
is revealed.

00:28:29.971 --> 00:28:31.745
To make that all work,

00:28:31.746 --> 00:28:35.815
on top of that we have what
we call the side-tree nodes

00:28:35.816 --> 00:28:38.530
which take kind of
all the information

00:28:38.531 --> 00:28:40.160
when there is an update, right,

00:28:40.161 --> 00:28:44.920
I got a new credit card
after the old one expired,

00:28:44.921 --> 00:28:48.120
I got a new driver's license or

00:28:48.121 --> 00:28:52.080
